window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-10-22T20:38:48.911580Z","count":14,"pages":[{"title":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting?","url":"/ai-assisted-ufo-sighting-investigation/","description":"AI-assisted UFO sighting investigation uses artificial intelligence, automated data searches and evidence comparison to determine what might explain a...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? AI assisted UFO sighting investigation uses artificial intelligence, automated data searches and evidence comparison to determine what might explain a... AI assisted UFO sighting investigation uses artificial intelligence, automated data searches and evidence comparison to determine what might explain a specific sighting. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation ai assisted ufo sighting investigation Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction AI can organise witness accounts, reconstruct timelines, identify possible matches in historical records and highlight contradictions between different sources. It can also help investigators recognise when an apparently unusual object is consistent with a satellite, aircraft, balloon or atmospheric phenomenon. The important distinction is between identifying a credible explanation and simply finding something that happened nearby. A coincidence is not proof, while an unsuccessful search does not establish that a sighting was extraordinary. NASA's 2023 independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) emphasised the need for better observations, reliable contextual information and scientific methods. These principles provide a foundation for using AI responsibly in UFO investigations. NASA Science How does an AI assisted UFO investigation work? A useful investigation begins with one specific event, not a general question about whether UFOs exist. Its purpose is to establish what was observed, reconstruct the circumstances and assess possible explanations using evidence that can be independently checked. The workflow combines human investigation with automated analysis. AI handles repetitive searching, information extraction and comparison, while investigators judge witness reliability, evidence quality and the physical plausibility of competing explanations. A practical investigation normally follows six stages: Illustrative overview 1. Record the sighting: Collect the original witness account, location, date, time, duration, direction of observation and any photographs or videos. 2. Standardise the information: Convert the report into a structured case file, identifying uncertain or missing details. 3. Reconstruct the event: Establish a timeline, viewing direction, possible movement and relevant geographical features. 4. Search for known explanations: Compare the observation with weather, astronomical objects, aircraft, drones, balloons, satellites and other recorded activity. 5. Compare historical cases: Find earlier sightings with similar characteristics and assess whether their explanations apply. 6. Evaluate the outcome: Rank plausible explanations, identify contradictions and document what remains unresolved. This broadly follows the investigative principles used by GEIPAN, the French national space agency's specialist UAP investigation service. GEIPAN uses a reproducible process involving witness testimony, case file creation, initial analysis, detailed investigation and classification. GEIPAN The advantage of automation is not that it replaces these stages. It makes them more consistent and allows investigators to examine contextual information that might otherwise be overlooked. Turning a witness report into a structured case file The quality of an AI investigation depends heavily on the information supplied at the beginning. A report stating that someone saw a bright object moving strangely provides too little detail for reliable identification. A structured case file should preserve the witness's original words while organising the factual elements into searchable fields. Case file field Information to record Date and time Calendar date, local time, time zone and estimated accuracy Location Observation coordinates, address or approximate area Witness position Whether stationary, walking, driving or travelling in an aircraft Duration How long the event lasted, including uncertainty Viewing direction Compass bearing, elevation above the horizon and changes over time Appearance Colour, shape, brightness, apparent size and number of objects Behaviour Movement, hovering, flashing, direction changes or disappearance Environmental conditions Cloud cover, visibility, wind and nearby lighting Supporting evidence Original photographs, videos, audio, radar or instrument records Witness information Number of observers, independent accounts and relevant viewing conditions These fields allow automated searches to be tied directly to the claimed observation. Separating observations from interpretations A particularly important task for AI is distinguishing what a witness actually saw from what they concluded. For example, consider the statement: “A silent, triangular spacecraft hovered over the houses before accelerating away.” The directly reported observations might instead be: Three lights appeared in a triangular arrangement. No sound was heard. The lights seemed stationary for approximately 20 seconds. Their apparent position then changed rapidly. The original interpretation should remain in the record, but it should not become an established fact. There may have been three separate lights rather than one triangular object. Silence does not establish the absence of an engine, and apparent acceleration does not necessarily indicate rapid movement through physical space. AI language models can help separate these elements, but investigators must review the results to ensure that the system has not altered or invented details. Preserving original evidence Photographs and videos should be retained in their original form, with the original filenames, timestamps and available metadata. AI enhanced versions may help reveal faint features, but enhancement can introduce artificial details. A sharpened edge or reconstructed texture should never be treated as new physical evidence. Where possible, investigators should also record camera settings, focal length, zoom, frame rate, digital stabilisation and whether an image has been cropped or compressed. NASA's UAP study highlighted the importance of sensor calibration and metadata when investigating unusual observations. Without information about how an image was captured, an apparent anomaly may be difficult to distinguish from an imaging artefact. NASA Science Which environmental checks matter most? Once the initial case file is complete, automation can search historical data covering the relevant time and place. The strongest checks are those capable of directly testing a reported object's position, movement, appearance or timing. Other environmental information may be useful but should receive less weight unless there is a clear mechanism linking it to the observation. Aircraft, drones and other aviation activity Aircraft should be among the first possibilities examined when a witness reports moving lights, hovering objects or unexplained formations. Flight tracking services such as ADS B Exchange can provide recorded positions for many aircraft. Its historical data products include archived aircraft location information that can be compared with a sighting's time and geographical coordinates. ADS B Exchange An automated investigation can compare: Recorded aircraft positions with the witness's viewing direction. Flight paths with the object's apparent movement. Aircraft altitude and distance with reported brightness or sound. Airport approaches, departures and holding patterns with repeated observations. Known aviation lighting patterns with the colours and flashing behaviour described. However, flight tracking records are not complete inventories of everything airborne. Coverage varies, and some aircraft or drones may not appear in accessible records. Consequently, finding a matching aircraft can provide strong supporting evidence, but finding no aircraft should not automatically exclude aviation. Drone activity requires separate attention because smaller drones often lack publicly accessible tracking histories. Public displays, authorised operations and local incident reports may provide useful supporting information. Satellites, planets and meteors Astronomical checks are especially valuable when objects appear as bright points, travel steadily without audible sound or seem to remain stationary. Satellite identification software can calculate whether a known object was predicted to pass through the relevant part of the sky. Services such as CelesTrak provide orbital information used for satellite tracking and trajectory prediction. The investigation should consider the satellite's predicted direction, elevation, visibility and timing. Historical orbital data is preferable to simply reversing today's satellite positions, because satellite orbits change and prediction accuracy is limited. CelesTrak Astronomical"},{"title":"Case Files","url":"/case-files/","description":"A useful UFO investigation starts by preserving witness accounts, checking photographs and reconstructing exactly when and where the sighting occurred.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting?","level":2,"text":"Case Files How Do You Turn a UFO Report Into Evidence? UFO Case Lab Case Files Case Files How Do You Turn a UFO Report Into Evidence? A useful UFO investigation starts by preserving witness accounts, checking photographs and reconstructing exactly when and where the sighting occurred. A useful UFO investigation starts by preserving witness accounts, checking photographs and reconstructing exactly when and where the sighting occurred. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation implementation pillar dataset evidence What to record from witnesses: exact words, dates, times, locations, viewing directions and uncertainty Reconstructing the sighting timeline and separating observed behaviour from witness interpretations Checking original photographs, video metadata and camera effects without introducing artificial details ai assisted ufo sighting investigation case files Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction A reliable UFO sighting case file turns an unusual observation into a documented event that can be investigated, checked and reassessed. The essential steps are to preserve the witness's original account, establish exactly when and where the sighting happened, reconstruct what was observed, secure any photographs or video, and distinguish verified facts from estimates and interpretations. The objective is not to prove or disprove an extraordinary explanation. It is to create a trustworthy record that allows investigators to test ordinary explanations, identify contradictions and recognise genuinely unresolved questions. Artificial intelligence (AI) can help extract information, organise timelines and identify missing evidence. However, its findings are only as dependable as the original material. This approach reflects the work of France's official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, which uses witness questionnaires, supporting evidence and documented investigations. NASA's 2023 UAP study similarly emphasised standardised observations, reliable sensor information and better quality data. GEIPAN How do you build a UFO case file step by step? A case file should be organised around one specific observation, even when several people witnessed it. Each statement, recording and contextual finding should remain traceable to its source. The following sequence provides a practical workflow. Illustrative overview 1. Create a case record. Assign a unique reference number and record when the report was received, who received it and whether the observation is first hand. 2. Preserve the initial testimony. Retain the witness's original description without rewriting unusual language or correcting apparent contradictions. 3. Collect essential sighting details. Record the date, time, location, duration, viewing direction, appearance, movement and observing conditions. 4. Reconstruct the event. Establish a chronological sequence showing when the object appeared, what happened during the sighting and how the observation ended. 5. Secure original evidence. Obtain photographs, video, audio, sketches and instrument records in their earliest available forms. 6. Check evidence quality. Examine timestamps, camera characteristics, missing footage, uncertain estimates and possible recording artefacts. 7. Document uncertainties and contradictions. Clearly distinguish direct observations, later recollections, investigator calculations and possible explanations. 8. Prepare the file for verification. Produce a structured summary that can support subsequent checks against aircraft, astronomical objects, satellites, weather and other possible causes. GEIPAN's published methodology provides a useful institutional model: it begins with testimony and case file creation before progressing to analysis, investigation and classification. GEIPAN The order matters. Beginning with an attractive explanation can influence subsequent questioning, while preserving the account first allows competing explanations to be examined against the same underlying record. What information should every UFO report contain? A useful report records both the event itself and the circumstances under which it was observed. The most important distinction is between details that can be independently verified and those that depend on the witness's perception or memory. Case file field What to record Why it matters Case identification Unique reference, intake date and investigator Keeps records and revisions traceable Date and time Date, local time, time zone and uncertainty Enables accurate comparison with external events Observation location Address, coordinates or approximate area Establishes the geographical reference point Witness circumstances Standing, walking, driving or travelling Helps assess apparent movement Duration Estimated start, finish and total observation time Constrains possible event matches Viewing direction Compass bearing, elevation and changes Allows reconstruction of the object's apparent path Appearance Colour, brightness, apparent size, shape and object count Preserves descriptive features without assuming identity Observed behaviour Motion, flashing, apparent acceleration and disappearance Provides a sequence of testable claims Surroundings Weather, visibility, obstacles and artificial lighting Identifies conditions affecting perception Supporting material Original recordings, sketches, photographs and instrument data Enables independent examination Witness relationships Number of witnesses and whether accounts were independent Helps assess corroboration Uncertainty Missing, estimated, contradictory or unverified details Prevents false precision These fields should not all be treated as equally reliable. A precise date supported by an original recording may be stronger than a remembered time supplied months later. Similarly, a compass bearing measured during a reconstruction is different from a direction recalled from memory. NASA's 2023 independent study highlighted the limitations of observations without reliable timing, location, sensor information and calibration. Such gaps can prevent researchers from making firm conclusions even where video evidence exists. NASA Science Record uncertainty rather than filling gaps An apparently incomplete record can still be valuable if its limitations are clear. For example, a witness might report an observation at approximately 21:30, but only remember that it occurred sometime between 21:15 and 21:45. Recording 21:30 as an exact timestamp would create misleading precision. A stronger case file would distinguish the original estimate from the possible interval and record how the time was established. Use the same approach for direction, duration, apparent size and distance. A missing value should remain unknown rather than being replaced with an AI generated estimate. How should witness testimony be collected? Witness interviews are often the most sensitive stage of UFO evidence collection. The investigator must obtain detailed information without unintentionally shaping the account. The UK College of Policing's guidance on non leading questioning [college.police.uk] college.police.uk College of Policing Non leading approach to questioning College of Policing Non leading approach to questioning Published: November 24, 2020 explains that suggestive questions can cause witnesses to report details they did not originally observe. Although the guidance concerns investigative interviewing generally, the same risk applies when questioning UFO witnesses. College of Policing Preserve the witness's exact words Begin with an open invitation such as: Please describe everything you remember seeing, from the moment you first noticed it until the sighting ended. Allow the witness to recount the event before introducing structured questions. The initial statement should preserve unusual descriptions, emotional reactions and uncertainty. Corrections or clarifications should be stored separately rather than silently replacing the first version. Consider the difference between these statements: Original observation: \"I saw a bright white light moving towards the rooftops.\" Witness interpretation: \"It looked as though a spacecraft was coming down.\" Investigator's inference: \"The object's apparent elevation decreased during the sighting.\" These are not interchangeable. The first describes what was seen, the second communicates the witness's interpretation, and the third attempts to express the observation in measurable terms. A reliable file keeps all three distinct. Ask neutral, specific questions After the uninterrupted account, useful questions include: Where were you standing when you first noticed the object? What were you doing immediately before the sighting? Which direction were you facing? What could you see around or behind the object? Did its appearance change while you watched? What made you look away or lose sight of it? How did you establish the time and duration? Did you discuss the sighting or see online reports before giving this account?"},{"title":"Witness Interviews","url":"/witness-interviews/","description":"A careful UFO witness interview preserves what people actually saw while reducing leading questions, shared memories and later reinterpretations.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Case Files","level":3,"text":"Witness Interviews Can UFO Witness Interviews Change What People Remember? Case Files Witness Interviews Witness Interviews Can UFO Witness Interviews Change What People Remember? A careful UFO witness interview preserves what people actually saw while reducing leading questions, shared memories and later reinterpretations. A careful UFO witness interview preserves what people actually saw while reducing leading questions, shared memories and later reinterpretations. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation critique risk bounded topic mechanism How to record a UFO witness's first account using neutral, open questions Why independent witness statements matter when several people report the same sighting How to separate direct observations, personal interpretations and later recollections ai assisted ufo sighting investigation case files witness interviews Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction Yes, UFO witness interviews can change what people remember. Leading questions, conversations between witnesses and exposure to suggested explanations can influence later recollections, even when everyone involved is acting sincerely. The safest approach is to obtain each witness's account independently, let them describe the sighting in their own words, ask neutral follow up questions and preserve the original account separately from later interpretations. For an AI assisted UFO investigation, this is particularly important. A witness's description of a light suddenly changing direction may be valuable evidence, but an interviewer who introduces words such as'craft','acceleration'or'impossible manoeuvre'can unintentionally turn an uncertain observation into a much stronger claim. Research on eyewitness memory and guidance from the UK's College of Policing support open questioning, early documentation and protection against information contamination. France's official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, also recognises the fragility of human testimony. College of Policing How to record a UFO witness's first account The first interview should establish what the person remembers before the investigator begins testing explanations. It should not be an interrogation designed to prove that the witness saw either an extraordinary object or something ordinary. The UK's [College of Policing investigative interviewing guidance] police.uk Open source on police.uk. recommends free recall: allowing witnesses to give their account with minimal interruption before moving towards more specific questions. Although developed for policing rather than UFO investigations, the approach provides a useful evidence based foundation. College of Policing A practical UFO witness interview follows seven stages. 1. Preserve the earliest description. Save the original email, message, voice recording or handwritten statement. Record when it was created and whether the witness had already discussed the sighting with anyone. 2. Explain the interview's purpose. Tell the witness that the aim is to understand their experience, not to judge them or establish a particular explanation. Make clear that uncertainty and'I don't know'are acceptable. 3. Invite an uninterrupted account. Begin with a neutral invitation such as,'Please describe everything you remember about what happened, starting just before you noticed anything unusual.' 4. Clarify the sequence. Ask what first attracted their attention, what they observed next, whether the appearance changed and how the sighting ended. 5. Explore observable details. Establish apparent colour, brightness, shape, direction, movement, duration and the witness's position. Ask how estimates were made rather than assuming they are measurements. 6. Document uncertainty and outside influences. Ask which details are clear, which are estimates and what the witness has heard or seen about the event since it happened. 7. Close without supplying an explanation. Invite corrections or additional information, explain how the account will be stored and preserve the recording or notes in their original form. With the witness's informed permission, an audio recording can retain wording, pauses and qualifications that a written summary may omit. Record the interview date, interviewer, participants and any interruptions. A transcript is useful for searching and analysis, but the recording should remain the reference. The US National Institute of Justice's [Eyewitness Evidence guide] National Institute of Justice Eyewitness Evidence: A Guide for Law Enforcement October 1, 1999. https://nij.ojp.gov/library/publications/eyewitness evidence guide law enforcement. Source panel: Citations. Accessed October 20, 2026 Open source on ojp.gov. similarly recommends open questions, avoiding guesses and leading prompts, allowing witnesses to draw what they remember, and documenting their recollections accurately. National Institute of Justice Neutral questions versus leading questions Small changes in wording can produce very different evidence. Leading question Better neutral question Did the UFO suddenly accelerate? How did its apparent movement change, if at all? Was the craft triangular? How would you describe its shape or outline? Did it move silently? What sounds, if any, did you notice? How high above the houses was it? Where did it appear in relation to the houses? Did it disappear instantly? What did you see when you last noticed it? Did you see the same three lights as the other witness? How many lights, if any, do you remember seeing? These distinctions are more than stylistic. In a landmark 1974 experiment, psychologists Elizabeth Loftus and John Palmer found that changing a verb in a question about a filmed car accident affected participants'speed estimates. Some participants later reported broken glass that had not appeared in the film. The study demonstrated how information introduced after an event can influence subsequent reports of memory. ScienceDirect The finding does not mean UFO witnesses are necessarily mistaken. It means interviewers should avoid supplying details that the witness has not independently introduced. A particularly important example concerns speed. A witness may describe a light crossing the sky in two seconds, but without reliable distance information, its physical speed cannot normally be calculated. Asking how fast'the craft'travelled risks converting an apparent angular movement into an unsupported claim about velocity. Instead, ask the witness to describe the movement against fixed landmarks, such as a rooftop, tree or distant hill. When sketches and memory prompts help A sketch can be more useful than repeated verbal questions, particularly when a witness struggles to describe an unfamiliar shape or movement. Ask the witness to draw the horizon, nearby landmarks, their viewing position and the object's apparent path. They should mark which features they remember directly and which are approximate. The College of Policing also discusses mental reinstatement of context, a technique in which witnesses are encouraged to recall their surroundings and circumstances at the time of an event. Its evidence review found that such techniques can improve the amount and accuracy of information recalled, although much of the supporting research used controlled experimental settings. College of Policing For a UFO sighting, a suitable prompt might be:'Think about where you were standing and what you were doing immediately before you looked up.' This is different from asking someone to imagine missing parts of the event. Investigators should not encourage witnesses to fill gaps, visualise hypothetical objects or repeatedly reconstruct details they cannot remember. A drawing produced after viewing another person's sketch or an online UFO illustration should be labelled accordingly. It may still be informative, but it is not equivalent to an independently produced first sketch. Why independent witness statements matter Several people reporting the same UFO sighting can strengthen an investigation, particularly when they observed it from different locations. But multiple accounts are not necessarily independent accounts. People often discuss an unusual event immediately afterwards. One person may describe a triangular shape, another may suggest a drone, and someone else may mention an apparent change in speed. By the time formal interviews begin, the witnesses may have developed a shared description that no longer reflects what each person originally noticed. This process is known as memory conformity. In a 2003 study, researchers Fiona Gabbert, Amina Memon and Kevin Allan showed pairs of participants different versions of the same event. After discussing what they had seen, 71% of participants in the discussion condition mistakenly recalled at least one detail acquired through that discussion. The experiment did not involve UFO sightings, and its percentage should not be treated as a prediction for real UFO witnesses. It nevertheless demonstrates that"},{"title":"Sighting Timeline","url":"/sighting-timeline/","description":"A UFO sighting timeline can reveal what happened and when, provided uncertain times, viewing directions and recording gaps remain visible.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Case Files","level":3,"text":"Sighting Timeline How Can You Reconstruct a UFO Sighting Accurately? Case Files Sighting Timeline Sighting Timeline How Can You Reconstruct a UFO Sighting Accurately? A UFO sighting timeline can reveal what happened and when, provided uncertain times, viewing directions and recording gaps remain visible. A UFO sighting timeline can reveal what happened and when, provided uncertain times, viewing directions and recording gaps remain visible. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation implementation bounded topic event time window Building a sequence from first appearance to disappearance using witness observations Checking reported times, viewing directions and apparent movement against recordings and landmarks Using AI to organise conflicting timestamps and identify gaps without inventing missing details ai assisted ufo sighting investigation case files sighting timeline Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction Reconstructing a UFO sighting accurately means arranging every reported observation, photograph, video clip and independent record into a chronological sequence while keeping uncertain times and missing evidence visible. The aim is not to produce a perfectly continuous story. It is to establish what can be verified, what witnesses remember and which parts of the event remain unknown. A reliable reconstruction starts with the first appearance, follows changes in position or behaviour, and ends with the last confirmed observation. Investigators then compare reported times with recording metadata, visible landmarks and other time stamped evidence. Artificial intelligence (AI) can organise these details, identify contradictions and suggest where to look for missing information. It cannot legitimately invent events between observations. This distinction is central to NASA's 2023 study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP), which emphasised accurate timing, sensor metadata and well calibrated observations as foundations for meaningful investigation. NASA Science How do you reconstruct a UFO sighting step by step? Begin with a working timeline rather than an explanation of what the object might have been. A witness who reports seeing a bright object at approximately 21:15, filming it briefly and watching it disappear has already provided several potentially useful events. However, those events may have different levels of timing accuracy. The following six step process turns such a report into a timeline that can be checked and revised. 1. Preserve the original account. Record the witness's exact description, including uncertain wording such as\"about ten minutes\"or\"shortly after sunset\". Do not replace approximate statements with exact times. 2. Divide the sighting into observable stages. Identify first appearance, changes in brightness or movement, interruptions, recording periods, and the final observation. 3. Collect independent time references. Examine original photographs, video files, messages, telephone records and other available evidence that might establish when particular stages occurred. 4. Check the viewing geometry. Record where the witness stood, which direction they faced, where the object appeared relative to landmarks and whether the observer or camera moved. 5. Reconcile timestamps and uncertainties. Convert times to a consistent reference, check device clocks and distinguish measured durations from estimates. 6. Build an annotated timeline. Give each event a time or time window, an evidence source, an uncertainty assessment and a note explaining any disagreement. France's official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, provides a useful model for this evidence first approach. Its investigation process centres on direct witness testimony, supported where possible by sketches, photographs, videos and other observations. GEIPAN Record separate stages, not one continuous movement A sighting often contains several distinct observations that should not automatically be treated as uninterrupted activity. For example, a witness may first notice a stationary light, look away to fetch a telephone, begin filming, observe apparent sideways movement and finally lose sight of the light behind a building. The interval spent retrieving the telephone is important. The investigator cannot assume the light remained stationary during that period, even if it appears in roughly the same part of the sky when filming begins. A useful reconstruction therefore distinguishes: First detection: When the witness first noticed the phenomenon, not necessarily when it first appeared. Initial behaviour: Its reported position, appearance and movement. Changes: Turning, brightening, dimming, splitting or apparent acceleration. Observation interruptions: Moments when the witness looked away or the object became obscured. Recorded intervals: Periods directly supported by photographs, video or instruments. Last detection: The final confirmed sighting, which may not be the moment the phenomenon physically disappeared. This structure avoids one of the most consequential mistakes in UFO investigations: treating the absence of observation as evidence that nothing changed. How should uncertain times appear in the timeline? A precise looking timestamp is not necessarily an accurate one. A camera may record seconds correctly while its internal clock is several minutes fast. Conversely, a witness may remember only an approximate clock time but accurately describe the order of events. The practical solution is to store time ranges and their sources, not just single timestamps. Consider the following fictional example of a sighting reported near a railway station. Event Reconstructed time Evidence and limitation First light noticed 21:12–21:15 Witness estimate Light appears stationary After first detection Witness account only Telephone recording begins 21:16:08, device time Original video metadata; clock not yet verified Light moves towards rooftop 18 seconds into recording Visible in video Light passes behind roofline 43 seconds into recording Visible in video Witness reports seeing it again Approximately 21:18 No recording Final sighting 21:18–21:21 Witness estimate This timeline contains several different kinds of information. The recording establishes an internal sequence of events, but its absolute clock time remains provisional. The witness's account establishes that another observation was reported after the recorded disappearance, but does not independently verify what happened during the gap. A defensible timeline preserves these differences instead of smoothing them into a single continuous flight path. Distinguish clock time from elapsed time Two separate questions need answering: What time did the event happen? This requires an accurate date, time zone and clock reference. How long did the event last? This may be established from video frame timing or estimated from witness recollection. These measurements should not be confused. A video can reliably establish that an object remained visible for 43 seconds even when the recording device's clock was incorrectly set. The US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) specifically addresses clock offsets in its guidance on CCTV video evidence. Its recommended approach involves comparing a recording system's clock with an external reference and documenting the difference. NIST For a UFO case, investigators should record the original device time, any measured clock correction and the resulting adjusted time separately. A simple rule is to retain the original value even after a correction. This allows another investigator to reproduce the calculation. Use consistent time zones A sighting at 22:00 in London cannot be compared directly with a flight record at 22:00 UTC without first checking whether British Summer Time was in effect. The case file should preserve the reported local time and record the corresponding Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), together with the date and applicable offset. Particular care is needed around daylight saving changes, when some local clock times can occur twice or not at all. An unknown time zone should be labelled unknown rather than silently assumed. How can photographs and video establish the sequence? Photographs and video often provide the strongest timing anchors in an incomplete UFO report, but only when their technical limitations are understood. A recording establishes what its camera captured during a particular interval. It does not automatically establish when the entire sighting began, whether the camera was pointed at the same object throughout, or what happened outside the frame. The Scientific Working Group on Digital Evidence (SWGDE), which publishes forensic guidance on electronic evidence, treats timeline reconstruction as a process of relating video, still images, audio and other records in chronological order. Its guidance also warns that differences between recording systems can cause synchronisation errors. SWGDE Preserve the original recording The"},{"title":"Sky Checks","url":"/sky-checks/","description":"AI can compare a UFO sighting with flight paths, satellite passes, astronomical positions and historical weather to test ordinary explanations.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting?","level":2,"text":"Sky Checks Was That UFO an Aircraft, Satellite or Something Else? UFO Case Lab Sky Checks Sky Checks Was That UFO an Aircraft, Satellite or Something Else? AI can compare a UFO sighting with flight paths, satellite passes, astronomical positions and historical weather to test ordinary explanations. AI can compare a UFO sighting with flight paths, satellite passes, astronomical positions and historical weather to test ordinary explanations. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation mechanism theme cluster mechanism Checking aircraft routes, airport activity, drones and aviation lights against the witness's reported movement Matching satellites, planets, meteors and space activity to the sighting's time and position in the sky Testing clouds, atmospheric optics, balloons and local conditions while distinguishing genuine matches from coincidences ai assisted ufo sighting investigation sky checks Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction Yes. Aircraft, satellites, astronomical objects and weather phenomena can explain many UFO sightings, and checking them against the exact time and location is one of the most effective ways to investigate an unusual light or object in the sky. The crucial question is not whether an ordinary object was nearby, but whether its position, movement, appearance and timing genuinely match the witness's account. AI assisted UFO investigation makes these comparisons faster. Software can reconstruct historical flight paths, calculate satellite positions, identify visible planets and retrieve weather records. It can then flag potential explanations and highlight where the evidence agrees or conflicts. The results are not always conclusive. Some aircraft are absent from public tracking services, satellite predictions have uncertainties, and weather observations may not capture conditions at the precise viewing location. A credible investigation therefore distinguishes a convincing identification from a possible explanation and an unresolved sighting. NASA's [2023 independent UAP study] NASA Science UAP June 16, 2022. https://science.nasa.gov/uap/. Source panel: Citations. Accessed October 20, 2026 Open source on nasa.gov. emphasised the importance of better observations, contextual data and scientific analysis. NASA Science Which explanation should investigators check first? The quickest starting point is to compare the reported behaviour with the characteristics of familiar objects. Different explanations leave different clues, and those clues determine which records an investigator should examine first. Possible explanation Typical appearance or behaviour Most useful comparison Aircraft Flashing lights, steady movement, apparent hovering when approaching head on Historical flight tracks, bearings, altitude and airport activity Drones Low altitude lights, hovering, turns and variable speed Local operations, eyewitness evidence and available drone identification records Satellites Steady lights moving across the sky, occasional bright flashes or groups of lights Orbital positions, viewing geometry and sunlight conditions Planets and bright stars Bright, apparently stationary or slowly shifting points, sometimes shimmering Astronomical position at the exact date and time Meteors and re entries Brief streaks, fragmentation, glowing trails or several bright objects Fireball reports, trajectory estimates and re entry records Balloons Slow drifting, occasional hovering or unexpected changes in apparent direction Wind at relevant altitudes and known launches Clouds and atmospheric optics Unusual shapes, luminous patches, rings, distortion or apparent movement Cloud observations, satellite imagery, temperature profiles and light source positions These are investigative starting points, not identification rules. For instance, a steady light might be a satellite, but it could also be a distant aircraft whose flashing lights are not visible. A glowing object that appears stationary could be a planet, a balloon or an aircraft approaching almost directly towards the observer. NASA's [guide to identifying UFOs and UAPs] NASA Science Identifying UFOs and UAPs December 1, 2013. https://science.nasa.gov/solar system/skywatching/night sky network/identifying ufos and uaps/. Source panel: Citations. Accessed October 20, 2026 Open source on nasa.gov. describes several of these ordinary sources of confusion, including Venus, aircraft, rockets, satellites, meteors and unusual clouds. NASA Science The most important distinction is between a behavioural resemblance and a verified match. A candidate becomes persuasive when its predicted position and motion agree with independent observations. Could an aircraft explain the reported movement? Aircraft are a particularly important explanation because their lighting, distance and viewing angle can make ordinary flight behaviour look unfamiliar. A distant aircraft approaching almost directly towards a witness may seem to hover for several minutes. Its apparent position changes very little even though it is travelling rapidly. As it turns, the visible lights and apparent speed may change suddenly. Landing lights can look like a single brilliant white object. Navigation lights and anti collision beacons can produce changing colours or flashing patterns, while atmospheric haze and cloud can obscure the aircraft itself. NASA's explanation of common unidentified lights specifically notes that the slow apparent movement of distant aircraft near the horizon can make them difficult to distinguish from bright planets. NASA Science How historical flight tracking helps Modern commercial aviation provides a useful investigative record. Aircraft equipped with Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) transmit position related information that can be collected by air traffic systems and independent receiver networks. For a UFO report, an AI assisted system can search historical flight records within the sighting's time window, retrieve candidate trajectories and calculate how each aircraft should have appeared from the witness's position. The comparison should consider: Time: Was the aircraft present during the reported observation, including reasonable uncertainty in the witness's clock? Bearing: Did the aircraft appear in the correct compass direction? Elevation: Was its height above the horizon compatible with the sighting? Motion: Did its apparent path, speed and changes of direction agree with the account? Lighting: Were the observed colours, flashes and brightness plausible for its orientation and distance? Duration: Could the aircraft remain visible for the reported length of time? A flight passing several kilometres from the witness is not automatically the explanation. What matters is the line of sight. A more distant aircraft directly ahead of the observer may be a stronger candidate than a nearer aircraft travelling across another part of the sky. The US Federal Aviation Administration's [ADS B technical guidance] Federal Aviation Administration ADS B FAQ. https://www.faa.gov/air traffic/technology/adsb/faq. Source panel: Citations. Accessed October 20, 2026 Open source on faa.gov. explains the surveillance system and its coverage and equipment limitations. Federal Aviation Administration A documented example: aircraft mistaken for distant objects The US All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO), which investigates unidentified anomalous phenomena for the US Government, has published a case involving objects recorded over the western United States. Its analysis combined video footage with commercial flight records. AARO concluded that the three distant objects corresponded to separate commercial aircraft, with their radar tracks aligning with the recorded objects. This illustrates why aircraft identification should not depend on visual appearance alone. Objects that are only small points in a sensor recording can become identifiable when the sensor's direction and the relevant flight trajectories are reconstructed together. AARO Why a blank flight map does not rule out aircraft Public flight tracking websites are useful, but they are not complete historical records of everything airborne. An aircraft may be missing because its equipment does not broadcast the expected data, a receiver network lacks coverage, or the public service has filtered or omitted relevant information. Military, police, emergency and privately operated aircraft may present additional difficulties, depending on their equipment and reporting arrangements. A more thorough investigation may therefore check airport movements, relevant airspace notices, published aviation activity, local aviation organisations and corroborating witness reports. No aircraft visible on a public flight map means only that no suitable aircraft was found in that dataset. It does not establish that the airspace was empty. What about drones? Drones can produce behaviours that ordinary fixed wing aircraft do not: prolonged hovering, abrupt changes of"},{"title":"Aircraft Checks","url":"/aircraft-checks/","description":"Historical flight records can reveal whether an aircraft matched a UFO's reported position, movement and timing, but missing tracks leave uncertainty.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Sky Checks","level":3,"text":"Aircraft Checks Was That UFO Actually an Aircraft? Sky Checks Aircraft Checks Aircraft Checks Was That UFO Actually an Aircraft? Historical flight records can reveal whether an aircraft matched a UFO's reported position, movement and timing, but missing tracks leave uncertainty. Historical flight records can reveal whether an aircraft matched a UFO's reported position, movement and timing, but missing tracks leave uncertainty. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation evidence bounded topic dataset evidence Why aircraft can appear to hover, accelerate or change colour Matching historical flight tracks to a witness's viewing direction, elevation and timing What missing flight records and the AARO commercial aircraft case reveal about identification limits ai assisted ufo sighting investigation sky checks aircraft checks Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction Yes, historical flight tracks can identify some UFO sightings as ordinary aircraft. By reconstructing where aircraft were flying at the time of a sighting, investigators can compare their positions, directions, altitudes and movements with what a witness reported. A strong match may explain an apparently hovering light, an unexpected change of direction or several mysterious objects moving together. The important qualification is that flight tracking records are incomplete. An aircraft may be absent from a public database because of limited receiver coverage, tracking equipment, privacy restrictions or gaps in archived data. Finding no aircraft on a tracking website does not prove that the sky was empty. For AI assisted UFO investigations, historical flight records are therefore a valuable source of independent evidence, not a definitive test on their own. The strongest identifications combine flight data with accurate witness timing, viewing direction, video evidence and the geometry of the observation. The US government's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has demonstrated this approach in a documented case involving distant commercial aircraft mistaken for unidentified objects. Its findings illustrate both the usefulness of flight track reconstruction and the importance of estimating an object's distance correctly. AARO Why can aircraft look like UFOs? An aircraft does not need to perform an unusual manoeuvre to appear mysterious. Its apparent movement depends on where the observer is standing, which direction the aircraft is travelling and how far away it is. A passenger aircraft travelling hundreds of miles per hour can look almost stationary when it is approaching the observer directly. Conversely, an aircraft crossing the observer's field of view may appear to move quickly even when travelling at a similar speed. This difference between actual movement and apparent movement is central to investigating reported UFO behaviour. Why an approaching aircraft can appear to hover Imagine standing outside at night and watching a bright white light above the horizon. For several minutes, it seems to remain in almost exactly the same position. Then it begins moving sideways and becomes noticeably dimmer. One possible explanation is an aircraft approaching along a route that points nearly towards the observer. During the initial approach, most of the aircraft's movement is towards the witness rather than across their field of view. Its angular position changes slowly, creating the impression that it is hovering. When the aircraft turns, its sideways movement becomes more obvious. The observer may interpret this as a sudden acceleration or a stationary object unexpectedly beginning to move. Distance also matters. An aircraft far away produces a smaller angular movement than a nearby aircraft travelling at the same speed across the observer's line of sight. Without reliable distance information, a witness cannot determine the aircraft's true speed simply by watching its movement across the sky. NASA's [2023 independent UAP study] NASA Science FINDING NASA, with its expertise in data calibra September 13, 2023. https://science.nasa.gov/wp content/uploads/2023/09/uap independent study team final report.pdf. Source panel: Citations. Accessed October 20, 2026 Open source on nasa.gov. emphasised that establishing distance is essential when assessing claims of unusual speed or acceleration. NASA Science Why aircraft lights can change colour or disappear Aircraft carry several lighting systems that may produce confusing appearances at night. The US Federal Aviation Administration's [Airplane Flying Handbook] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. describes the conventional arrangement of red lights on the left wing, green lights on the right wing and white lights towards the rear. Aircraft may also display flashing anti collision lights and powerful landing lights. FAA The combination visible to an observer changes with the aircraft's orientation. A witness might initially see a brilliant white landing light, followed by a red or green navigation light as the aircraft turns. At a considerable distance, the aircraft's body may be invisible, leaving only a point of light. Atmospheric haze, thin cloud and camera exposure can further alter the apparent brightness or colour. These effects can account for reports involving lights that: Remain almost motionless before moving sideways. Change from white to red or green. Appear to brighten, dim or vanish. Seem to accelerate without an obvious change in physical speed. Look like separate lights that later merge or become difficult to distinguish. None of these observations establishes that an aircraft was responsible. They indicate which ordinary mechanisms should be tested against historical flight records. The key question is whether a particular aircraft's recorded movements would have produced the reported appearance from the witness's position. How can historical flight tracks be matched to a UFO sighting? Historical flight tracking works by reconstructing aircraft movements during a specific period and comparing those movements with the witness's view of the sky. The process is more demanding than finding a nearby aircraft on a map. An aircraft can be geographically close to a witness but appear in a completely different direction. Another aircraft, much farther away, may align almost perfectly with the reported UFO. A reliable comparison therefore requires three dimensional viewing geometry. Start with the witness's exact time and position The investigation begins with a structured sighting record. At minimum, the case should establish the date, observation time, location and approximate direction of the object. Ideally, it should also contain the object's apparent height above the horizon, duration, movement, colour and any photographic or video evidence. The distinction between local time and Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) is particularly important. Historical aviation datasets commonly use UTC. A sighting reported at 21:30 British Summer Time corresponds to 20:30 UTC. Searching the wrong hour can produce an entirely different set of aircraft. The investigator should also record how the time was obtained. A timestamp embedded in an original video may be more useful than a time reconstructed from memory, although camera clocks can themselves be incorrect. NASA's UAP study specifically identified accurate acquisition times, sensor locations and instrument metadata as necessary for meaningful analysis. NASA Compare direction and elevation, not just proximity A historical aircraft record may contain latitude, longitude, altitude, speed, heading and observation time. From these values, software can estimate where the aircraft would have appeared in the witness's sky. Two measurements are especially useful: Azimuth describes the compass direction from the observer towards the aircraft. For example, 90° represents east and 270° represents west. Elevation describes the angle above the horizon. An elevation of 0° is at the horizon, while 90° is directly overhead. Together, these measurements provide a more meaningful comparison than geographical distance alone. Suppose a witness reports a bright object approximately 15° above the western horizon. Historical data show two aircraft operating in the area. One aircraft is relatively nearby but would have appeared high in the southern sky. The other is much farther away but would have appeared approximately 15° above the western horizon. The second aircraft is the stronger candidate, despite being more distant. This is an illustrative example rather than a reconstruction of a documented sighting. It demonstrates why matching the witness's line of sight is more important than simply identifying the nearest flight. Aircraft altitude must also be handled carefully. Flight trackers may report barometric altitude, which is based on atmospheric pressure, rather than the aircraft's exact height above the terrain. For precise low elevation comparisons, investigators may need pressure corrections, terrain elevation"},{"title":"Starlink Sightings","url":"/starlink-sightings/","description":"Starlink satellites can resemble coordinated UFO formations, but their predicted paths and visibility must match the reported lights.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Sky Checks","level":3,"text":"Starlink Sightings Was That Line of UFO Lights Starlink? Sky Checks Starlink Sightings Starlink Sightings Was That Line of UFO Lights Starlink? Starlink satellites can resemble coordinated UFO formations, but their predicted paths and visibility must match the reported lights. Starlink satellites can resemble coordinated UFO formations, but their predicted paths and visibility must match the reported lights. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation mechanism bounded topic mechanism Why newly deployed Starlink satellites appear as moving lines of lights Checking satellite positions, sunlight and viewing angles against a reported sighting When satellite predictions support an identification and when the evidence remains inconclusive ai assisted ufo sighting investigation sky checks starlink sightings Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction A line of bright, evenly spaced lights moving silently across the night sky could very well be a train of Starlink satellites. These spacecraft, launched by SpaceX to provide satellite internet, can appear as a striking procession of glowing dots, particularly during the days following a launch. Their unusual appearance has repeatedly prompted UFO reports from members of the public and experienced pilots. The strongest way to identify Starlink is to match the reported lights with a specific satellite group's predicted position, direction, timing and visibility. A resemblance alone is not proof. Starlink sightings are particularly suitable for AI assisted UFO investigation because satellite orbits can be reconstructed mathematically. Automated systems can compare witness accounts, launch records, orbital data and sunlight conditions to determine whether a known satellite group offers a convincing explanation. The distinction matters: some sightings can be identified with considerable confidence, while others remain uncertain because the observation is incomplete or the historical tracking data is insufficient. NASA recognises satellites as a source of UFO misidentifications, and the US government's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) has investigated several cases involving Starlink. NASA Science Why do Starlink satellites look like a line of UFOs? Starlink satellites are launched in groups rather than individually. Following deployment, spacecraft from the same launch initially occupy closely related orbits. Seen from the ground, they can resemble a string of illuminated beads travelling across the sky. The lights are not navigation lamps or deliberate signals. They are sunlight reflected from satellite surfaces, including antennas, solar arrays and other components. The effect is particularly noticeable when the observer is in darkness but the satellites are still illuminated by the Sun. Why newly launched satellites appear together Immediately after deployment, satellites from the same launch are relatively close together along their orbital paths. Although they travel at enormous speeds around Earth, their similar motion makes them appear to maintain a coordinated formation. Several characteristics make the display distinctive: A recognisable line: Multiple points of light follow approximately the same route across the sky. Similar movement: The lights generally travel in the same direction at comparable apparent speeds. Little or no flashing: Individual satellites usually appear as steady white points, although their brightness can change. Silent passage: Satellites orbit far above the atmosphere and produce no audible engine noise for ground observers. Gradual separation: As the spacecraft manoeuvre into their intended orbits, the compact procession becomes more spread out. The formation is temporary. Starlink satellites use onboard propulsion to adjust their orbits after deployment, progressively increasing their separation. This is why a newly launched group may create an impressive procession while the same satellites become much harder to recognise as a group later. SpaceX's own explanation of satellite brightness describes the distinctive string of pearls appearance and explains why spacecraft can be more conspicuous during the orbit raising phase than after reaching their operational positions. Updates Why the lights are most visible around twilight A satellite does not need to be directly overhead to become visible. It needs to be above the observer's horizon, illuminated by the Sun and bright enough to stand out against the surrounding sky. Shortly after sunset, the ground may already be dark while a satellite hundreds of kilometres above it remains in sunlight. A similar arrangement occurs before sunrise. Illustrative geometry, not to scale: satellites can reflect sunlight towards an observer after sunset even though the ground is already dark. As the satellites travel into Earth's shadow, their reflected light can disappear. To someone watching without knowing the orbital geometry, several lights fading out one after another may look like objects switching off or vanishing. The reverse can occur when spacecraft emerge from shadow. Different satellites in the same group may also vary in brightness because of their orientation, surface reflectivity and viewing angle. SpaceX's satellite brightness guidance explains these geometric effects, while the satellite prediction service Heavens Above cautions that brightness estimates are inherently approximate. Starlink A common misconception is that Starlink satellites must always form a perfectly straight, equally spaced line. In practice, their apparent spacing can vary, individual satellites may be too faint to see, and a group can look more compact or spread out depending on its age and the observer's viewing angle. What real UFO reports have turned out to involve Starlink? Starlink is not merely a hypothetical explanation for unusual lights. Published investigations have connected particular sightings to known satellite activity. The August 2022 Pacific Ocean pilot sightings One especially informative case occurred on 10 August 2022, when five pilots aboard two commercial aircraft over the Pacific Ocean reported unusual lights. The incident attracted attention because it involved trained aviation observers and included photographs and video. A 2024 research paper by Douglas J. Buettner and colleagues, Enhancing Space Situational Awareness to Mitigate Risk, reconstructed the observations using several independent sources. The researchers combined the reported sighting circumstances with aircraft position data from Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B), which records aircraft movements, and orbital information for Starlink satellites launched that same day. By reconstructing the geometry from the aircraft's position, the study showed how the recently deployed Starlink group could account for the unusual observations. The case demonstrates an important investigative principle: a sighting can be genuinely puzzling to its witnesses yet still have a technically testable, ordinary explanation. The investigators did not rely solely on visual resemblance. They compared satellite movement and viewing geometry against the recorded circumstances. arXiv The Minehead lights in February 2023 A more familiar public example occurred in Minehead, Somerset, where residents reported seeing a trail of lights moving across the night sky. Local reporting described photographs and video taken by witness Marist Durham, alongside online discussion about whether the objects might be UFOs. The display was identified as Starlink satellites, illustrating how a conspicuous orbital procession can generate several reports from the same area before its source becomes known. Unlike a single ambiguous point of light, a satellite train may be visible to numerous witnesses across a broad region, producing apparently corroborating accounts that all originate from the same ordinary event. West Somerset Free Press What official UAP investigations have found In December 2024, AARO published an information paper titled Correlations of Starlink Satellite Flaring with UAP Observations. The paper examined how reflected sunlight from satellites can produce unusual appearances, including flashes and other brightness changes that may be mistaken for unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP). AARO's director also described investigations in which the timing, direction and location of observations were compared with Starlink flares, allowing analysts to identify a satellite explanation for several reports. These cases broaden the lesson beyond the classic straight line satellite train. Starlink can sometimes produce brief, unexpectedly bright observations even when individual spacecraft do not appear as an obvious procession. AARO However, these findings do not mean that every group of unexplained lights is Starlink. Each proposed identification must still survive comparison with the actual event. How can you check whether Starlink was visible during a sighting? The most"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases/","description":"AI can compare earlier UFO cases and rank possible explanations, but missing records, weak matches and uncertain evidence can leave a sighting unresolved.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting?","level":2,"text":"Unresolved Cases When Should a UFO Sighting Stay Unexplained? UFO Case Lab Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases When Should a UFO Sighting Stay Unexplained? AI can compare earlier UFO cases and rank possible explanations, but missing records, weak matches and uncertain evidence can leave a sighting unresolved. AI can compare earlier UFO cases and rank possible explanations, but missing records, weak matches and uncertain evidence can leave a sighting unresolved. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation critique risk decision cluster policy intervention Finding comparable historical UFO reports and testing whether earlier explanations fit the new sighting Ranking candidate explanations by timing, location, observed behaviour, independent corroboration and conflicting evidence Distinguishing identified, plausible, weak and unresolved outcomes without treating missing data as proof of something extraordinary ai assisted ufo sighting investigation unresolved cases Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction A UFO sighting should remain unexplained when the available evidence cannot support a reliable identification, even after reasonable investigation of known possibilities. That does not mean the object was extraordinary, extraterrestrial or operating beyond known physics. It means investigators cannot establish what caused the observation with sufficient confidence. The crucial distinction is between a sighting that cannot be explained because information is missing and one that remains unusual despite substantial evidence and careful testing. Artificial intelligence can help distinguish these outcomes by comparing historical UFO reports, ranking possible explanations and detecting contradictions. However, a convincing AI generated match is not necessarily a correct identification. NASA's 2023 study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) emphasised that limited high quality observations make firm scientific conclusions difficult. The appropriate response to insufficient evidence is to preserve uncertainty, not manufacture an answer. NASA Science When should investigators leave a UFO sighting unexplained? The decision should reflect both the quality of the evidence and how closely any proposed explanation matches the observation. A useful classification system separates cases into five outcomes: Outcome What the evidence establishes Appropriate decision Identified Reliable, independent information supports a specific object or phenomenon Close with a documented explanation Plausibly explained A known cause matches the main observations, but confirmation is incomplete Record the leading explanation and its uncertainty Weak match An explanation shares some characteristics but has significant gaps or contradictions Keep the hypothesis tentative Unresolved — insufficient data Missing or unreliable information prevents a meaningful comparison Record the evidence limitations without claiming an anomaly Unresolved — investigated Adequate evidence supports analysis, but no tested explanation accounts for the observations satisfactorily Retain as unexplained and eligible for reassessment This distinction closely resembles the approach used by GEIPAN, the UFO investigation service associated with France's national space agency, CNES. GEIPAN identifies cases under categories A and B when an explanation is established or considered probable. Category C covers observations that cannot be identified because information is insufficient. Category D covers observations left unidentified after investigation. Its more detailed classification also distinguishes D1 and D2 according to the strength and unusual nature of the remaining evidence. GEIPAN The distinction matters because an absence of information is not the same as positive evidence of unusual behaviour. A brief video of an unidentified light might remain in the insufficient data category because there is no reliable viewing direction, distance or recording time. By contrast, an observation supported by calibrated instruments and independent witnesses may justify more extensive investigation if the strongest conventional explanations conflict with measured facts. Neither outcome establishes an extraordinary origin. How many sightings remain unexplained? GEIPAN's published figures, updated to 31 March 2026, classify approximately 66.5% of its cases as explained or probably explained, 30.3% as unidentified because of insufficient information, and 3.2% as unexplained after investigation. These percentages describe GEIPAN's case records, not all UFO sightings worldwide. GEIPAN The contrast is revealing. Cases lacking enough information substantially outnumber those retained as unexplained following investigation. For AI assisted investigations, this suggests that the first decision should not be whether an observation looks extraordinary. It should be whether the available evidence is strong enough to support a meaningful conclusion. Can earlier UFO cases explain a new sighting? Historical UFO reports provide valuable comparisons because apparently unfamiliar observations often resemble previously investigated events. AI can search archived descriptions for similar appearances, movement patterns, durations and observing conditions. The purpose is not merely to find an earlier report that sounds alike. It is to establish whether the explanation reached in the earlier case could reasonably account for the new observation. Comparing behaviour rather than appearance alone Consider a hypothetical report of three bright lights travelling silently across the sky before disappearing in sequence. An automated search might retrieve earlier accounts involving satellites, aircraft formations, drones, balloons and atmospheric effects. Several matches could look convincing. Their investigative value depends on more specific comparisons. Satellite explanation: Does the observation time and viewing direction correspond to an actual orbital pass? Could the reported disappearance result from the satellites entering Earth's shadow? Aircraft explanation: Were aircraft operating in the relevant airspace, and would their lighting and flight paths match the reported pattern? Drone explanation: Is the movement compatible with small unmanned aircraft, and is there independent evidence of activity at the location? Balloon explanation: Could recorded winds and the likely viewing distance account for the movement and apparent formation? Perception explanation: Could apparent changes in speed or direction arise from viewing geometry, limited visual references or camera movement? Historical similarity helps identify hypotheses worth testing. It cannot establish that the same cause occurred twice. GEIPAN's methodology explicitly assesses possible known physical and perceptual causes while considering how well each hypothesis accounts for the reported observation. GEIPAN The historical comparison trap An archived case may resemble a new sighting closely while remaining unsuitable as evidence for its explanation. For example, a 1990s report of a bright triangular formation might be associated with aircraft. A newer report describing a similar formation could involve drones, satellites or another cause entirely. The common description does not make the underlying events equivalent. Historical databases also contain uneven reporting standards. Some records preserve precise dates, directions and original evidence, while others contain only brief witness summaries. Investigators should therefore distinguish between: Cases with independently verified explanations. Cases with credible but unconfirmed explanations. Cases that were never conclusively assessed. Repeated or copied versions of the same original account. An AI similarity system that treats these records equally could give unsupported explanations excessive weight. A particularly important safeguard is to prevent circular reasoning. If an earlier report was labelled a satellite sighting merely because it resembled other presumed satellite reports, that label should not become independent confirmation when the new case is assessed. When historical comparisons change the verdict A useful historical comparison contributes more than a familiar description. Suppose an earlier sighting was explained through a combination of satellite position calculations, witness viewing direction and photographic timing. That earlier investigation provides a testable mechanism. The new sighting can be checked against equivalent orbital and observational conditions. If those conditions agree, the satellite explanation becomes stronger. If the predicted satellite positions conflict with the witness's recorded direction, the historical resemblance should carry much less weight. This is where AI assisted comparison is particularly useful: it can retrieve the earlier explanation, identify the conditions required for it to work, and highlight which of those conditions are satisfied or contradicted by the new case."},{"title":"GEIPAN Cases","url":"/geipan-cases/","description":"GEIPAN's classifications distinguish UFO sightings with too little evidence from those that remain unidentified after detailed investigation.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Unresolved Cases","level":3,"text":"GEIPAN Cases Why Do Some UFO Sightings Remain Unexplained After Investigation? Unresolved Cases GEIPAN Cases GEIPAN Cases Why Do Some UFO Sightings Remain Unexplained After Investigation? GEIPAN's classifications distinguish UFO sightings with too little evidence from those that remain unidentified after detailed investigation. GEIPAN's classifications distinguish UFO sightings with too little evidence from those that remain unidentified after detailed investigation. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation evidence single dataset dataset evidence How GEIPAN distinguishes insufficient evidence from genuinely unexplained UFO sightings What separates category C cases from investigated D1 and D2 cases What GEIPAN's case statistics reveal about unexplained sightings and the limits of AI classification ai assisted ufo sighting investigation unresolved cases geipan cases Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction France's official UFO investigation service, GEIPAN, leaves some sightings unexplained because no tested explanation adequately accounts for the available evidence. Crucially, it distinguishes these cases from reports that cannot be identified simply because essential information is missing. GEIPAN operates within France's national space agency, CNES, and investigates reports of unidentified aerospace phenomena, commonly called UFOs or UAPs. Its classification system separates identified sightings, probable explanations, insufficiently documented reports and observations that remain unidentified after investigation. This distinction matters because an unexplained sighting is not automatically evidence of extraordinary technology. A report may resist identification because witnesses cannot reliably estimate distance, photographs lack context, or available records cannot establish what was in the sky. GEIPAN's approach offers a useful model for AI assisted UFO investigation: measure the strength of the evidence separately from the difficulty of explaining it. GEIPAN How GEIPAN distinguishes insufficient evidence from unexplained sightings GEIPAN uses a classification methodology refined in 2008. Rather than judging a sighting solely by how unusual it sounds, investigators assess two characteristics: the consistency of the evidence and the unexplained strangeness remaining after conventional explanations have been considered. Consistency concerns the quantity and reliability of available information. Investigators consider the precision of witness accounts, supporting photographs, the number of witnesses, whether witnesses are independent and whether different accounts agree. Residual strangeness describes how poorly the strongest known explanation fits the observation. GEIPAN evaluates candidate explanations involving natural phenomena, human activity and perceptual effects. An observation that initially seems extraordinary may become ordinary once its circumstances are reconstructed. GEIPAN's official methodology describes a strangeness measure between zero and one, with values above 0.5 indicating that no proposed explanation is sufficiently convincing. However, high strangeness alone is not enough to establish a category D case: the evidence must also be sufficiently consistent. This is an investigative assessment, not a statistically calibrated probability that an object has an extraordinary origin. GEIPAN Consider two hypothetical reports. One witness describes a bright object moving rapidly but cannot establish the observation time or viewing direction. Another reports an unusual object whose timing and position are independently corroborated, yet aircraft, satellite and astronomical checks produce no satisfactory match. Both reports may remain unidentified, but their evidential meaning differs. The first cannot be reliably tested; the second has evidence against which explanations can be evaluated. GEIPAN's categories are designed to preserve precisely this distinction. What separates category C from D1 and D2 cases? GEIPAN's categories represent different investigative outcomes rather than a simple scale of how mysterious a sighting appears. Classification Meaning Evidential significance A Identified Evidence supports a definite explanation B Probably identified A conventional explanation is convincing, although definitive proof is missing C Insufficient information The available evidence cannot support a reliable conclusion D1 Unidentified after investigation A moderately strange observation with sufficient consistency to retain as unexplained D2 Unidentified after investigation A highly strange observation supported by stronger, more consistent evidence GEIPAN's official English language FAQ illustrates D1 with cases involving a single witness or no photograph, and D2 with cases supported by several independent witnesses and photographs. These are examples, not rigid requirements: classification depends on the overall strength of the evidence and the remaining difficulty of explanation. geipan.fr The distinction is particularly important for category C. A case can sound extremely unusual yet still be unsuitable for detailed analysis. An unidentified light recorded without a reliable date, location or viewing direction cannot meaningfully be matched against aircraft movements, satellite passes or astronomical conditions. Category D requires a different judgement. GEIPAN's published methodology states that D1 and D2 classifications involve field investigations, witness meetings and cognitive interviews intended to recover and assess observational details. Expert consultation can also contribute to the final decision. Even this more intensive process cannot guarantee identification. A witness may describe an event accurately while the physical evidence remains too limited to establish its cause. Equally, an apparently consistent account may contain mistaken assumptions about speed, distance or motion. GEIPAN therefore allows classifications to be revised when new evidence becomes available. A D case is an unresolved investigative outcome, not a permanent declaration that ordinary explanations are impossible. cnes geipan.fr What GEIPAN's statistics reveal about unexplained sightings GEIPAN's published case statistics show that genuinely unexplained investigations represent a small minority of its classified records. Cases lacking sufficient evidence are much more common. GEIPAN published case statistics, 11 August 2026 Identified or probably identified 2,260 cases Insufficient data 1,015 cases Unexplained after investigation 106 cases Categories A/B Category C Category D 3,381 classified, published cases. Percentages are rounded. Source: [GEIPAN official statistics] geipan.fr Open source on geipan.fr. Published: August 11, 2026 . GEIPAN The striking comparison is between categories C and D. There are almost ten times as many insufficiently documented cases as cases remaining unexplained after investigation. These figures should not be interpreted as the probability that a randomly observed UFO is extraordinary. GEIPAN's database contains cases submitted to and processed by a particular organisation. Its published statistics exclude files still being investigated, and some straightforward reports are resolved without publication. The proportions also change as investigations are completed and older cases are reconsidered. GEIPAN's earlier institutional account reported that 50 previously unexplained cases had been reinvestigated and explained in 2017. This demonstrates how accumulated expertise, better records and new analytical methods can change historical classifications. GEIPAN GEIPAN's searchable archive makes these distinctions visible at the individual case level. For example, its records list the 8 August 2012 observation at Volx as D1, while the 8 April 2025 observation at Lugny Champagne is classified C. The labels indicate different evidential outcomes; they do not establish that the Volx observation involved an extraordinary object or that the Lugny Champagne witness observed nothing. GEIPAN Why investigation and AI cannot always produce an answer GEIPAN's own explanations identify several practical obstacles: collecting decisive details from witnesses, comparing competing hypotheses, involving suitable specialists and working within limited investigative resources. Human perception is especially important. A distant light may appear to move unexpectedly because of viewing conditions or perceptual effects. Without a reliable distance estimate, apparent speed cannot establish actual speed. Investigators therefore need to distinguish what a witness directly observed from what the witness inferred. GEIPAN GEIPAN can consult external specialists and records concerning weather, aviation, astronomical conditions, satellites and space debris. These checks can identify conventional causes that would otherwise remain obscure. Yet the absence of a matching record is not necessarily evidence that no conventional object was present:"},{"title":"Conflicting Evidence","url":"/conflicting-evidence/","description":"A convincing UFO explanation can fail when reliable timing, position or movement evidence contradicts the proposed cause.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Unresolved Cases","level":3,"text":"Conflicting Evidence What If the Best UFO Explanation Contradicts the Evidence? Unresolved Cases Conflicting Evidence Conflicting Evidence What If the Best UFO Explanation Contradicts the Evidence? A convincing UFO explanation can fail when reliable timing, position or movement evidence contradicts the proposed cause. A convincing UFO explanation can fail when reliable timing, position or movement evidence contradicts the proposed cause. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation critique risk single mechanism mechanism How verified timing, viewing direction and movement contradictions can rule out a proposed UFO identification Why historical UFO similarities and high AI confidence scores cannot outweigh contradictory measurements When unresolved contradictions justify an unexplained verdict rather than a weak or plausible identification ai assisted ufo sighting investigation unresolved cases conflicting evidence Updated 2026 10 22 Introduction A proposed UFO explanation should be rejected when reliable evidence contradicts something essential about the proposed object or event. An aircraft cannot explain a sighting if its verified position, timing and flight path are incompatible with the observation. A satellite cannot account for a light travelling in a demonstrably different direction. A balloon identification becomes doubtful if accurately measured movement cannot be reconciled with the relevant winds. The strength of that conclusion depends on the quality of the conflicting evidence. A calibrated recording or independently verified position carries more weight than an approximate recollection. Apparent contradictions caused by clock errors, viewing angles or camera effects must also be investigated before dismissing an explanation. For AI assisted UFO investigations, the central rule is that a strong resemblance is not enough when dependable measurements disagree. Rejecting the leading explanation may justify leaving the sighting unexplained, but it does not establish an extraordinary origin. NASA's [2023 independent UAP study] science.nasa.gov Open source on nasa.gov. supports this evidence led approach, emphasising the importance of reliable observations, sensor characteristics and independent measurements when investigating unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP). NASA Science Which contradictions can rule out a UFO explanation? A convincing identification must account for the observation as a whole, rather than matching one attractive detail. For example, an AI system might identify a satellite because a witness described a bright, silent object moving steadily across the night sky. That resemblance provides a useful hypothesis. However, if an accurately timestamped recording shows the object travelling in a direction incompatible with the satellite's calculated position and trajectory, the identification fails. The same principle applies to aircraft, drones, balloons, astronomical objects and atmospheric phenomena. The investigator must distinguish between a minor discrepancy that can reasonably be explained and a contradiction that makes the proposed identification physically incompatible with the evidence. Three kinds of contradiction are particularly important. Timing: was the proposed object actually there? Time is often the quickest way to eliminate an otherwise convincing explanation. Suppose a witness reports a bright object at 21:14, and an automated satellite search finds a visually similar pass at 21:42. If the recording time is independently verified and the satellite's predicted position is reliable, that satellite cannot explain the recorded event. However, a phone clock set incorrectly, a recording exported with altered metadata or a witness estimating the time from memory could account for the discrepancy. Before rejecting the candidate, investigators should establish whether the reported time is: Independently verified through an original recording, synchronised clock or corroborating record. A device timestamp whose time zone and clock accuracy have been checked. An approximate witness recollection with a wider uncertainty range. A time discrepancy is decisive only when the plausible timing ranges do not overlap. This is particularly relevant when AI searches satellite catalogues, flight histories or launch records. An exact looking database match is not proof that the object was visible at the witness's location and time. Position: was it in the right part of the sky? A proposed explanation must also occupy a position consistent with the observation. If a witness filmed a light above a recognisable building, investigators can use the building's location, the camera position and the image geometry to estimate the viewing direction. That direction can then be compared with the predicted position of an aircraft, planet or satellite. Consider an aircraft identified by a flight tracking system. Its recorded position might place it north west of the observer while a calibrated video establishes that the unexplained light was south east. If the discrepancy exceeds the combined positional and measurement uncertainties, that particular aircraft can be ruled out. But position checks have important limitations. Camera orientation may be unknown, electronic compasses can be inaccurate, and a witness's description of a compass direction may be approximate. A photograph also records angular position rather than distance. An object appearing immediately above a distant landmark may be much closer than the landmark itself. Consequently, the investigation must test whether the apparent positional contradiction survives realistic changes in camera orientation, observer location and estimated distance. Movement: could the proposed object behave that way? Movement contradictions can be more informative than appearance. An aircraft, balloon and satellite might all appear as a small white point in a recording. Their possible movements, however, differ considerably. A proposed identification becomes questionable when measured behaviour cannot be reproduced by the candidate under the recorded conditions. For example, a passive balloon explanation may fail if reliable observations establish sustained movement strongly against the winds at the balloon's actual altitude, beyond plausible atmospheric uncertainty. A satellite identification may fail if a properly reconstructed trajectory requires movement inconsistent with the satellite's orbit. The important qualification is that apparent movement is not necessarily physical movement. Camera rotation, zoom, tracking systems, perspective and motion parallax can make ordinary objects appear to accelerate, hover or travel at extraordinary speeds. NASA's UAP study specifically highlights the need to understand sensor limitations and optical effects before drawing conclusions about unusual behaviour. NASA Science The correct test is therefore whether the measured movement contradicts the explanation after accounting for the observing equipment and viewing geometry. What real UFO investigations reveal about conflicting evidence Two documented investigations illustrate why apparently compelling observations must be tested against reconstructed movement and why disproving unusual behaviour is different from identifying the object itself. The 2015 Go Fast video: apparent speed versus measured speed In January 2015, a US Navy F/A 18F recorded an unidentified object over the Atlantic Ocean near Florida using an infrared camera. The footage appeared to show an object travelling rapidly above the ocean. In February 2025, the US Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) published a detailed reconstruction. Its analysis placed the object approximately 13,000 feet above sea level, rather than close to the ocean surface. It estimated wind compensated movement ranging from approximately 5 to 92 miles per hour, depending on the assumed aircraft heading. AARO concluded with high confidence that the object did not demonstrate anomalous speed. It attributed the impression of rapid movement to motion parallax, where the movement of the observing aircraft changes the object's apparent motion against the background. AARO The significant distinction is between what the video initially seemed to show and what the reconstruction supported. The interpretation of an object travelling at extraordinary speed was inconsistent with AARO's calculated geometry and movement. Yet AARO did not claim to have definitively identified the physical object. Its published methodology also acknowledged limitations, including missing original recording data and uncertainty about the aircraft's exact position and heading. The appropriate lesson is not that every unusual video can be explained through parallax. It is that a striking visual interpretation can be rejected without resolving every question about the object. The 2013 Puerto Rico video: an apparent split and water entry On 26 April 2013, an"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-assisted-ufo-sigh-96e9bc-index/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of AI assisted UFO sighting investigation, covering Sky Checks, Case Files and Unresolved Cases.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting?","level":2,"text":"All Topics AI assisted UFO sighting investigation: All Topics All Topics All Topics AI assisted UFO sighting investigation: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of AI assisted UFO sighting investigation, covering Sky Checks, Case Files and Unresolved Cases. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation Explore Topics in AI assisted UFO sighting investigation The following pages expand on the main AI assisted UFO sighting investigation page and cover its key branches in more detail. Sky Checks Case Files Unresolved Cases"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-assisted-ufo-sigh-96e9bc-ufo/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Sky Checks, covering Aircraft Checks and Starlink Sightings.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Sky Checks","level":3,"text":"All Topics Sky Checks: All Topics All Topics All Topics Sky Checks: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Sky Checks, covering Aircraft Checks and Starlink Sightings. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation Explore Topics in Sky Checks The following pages expand on the main Sky Checks page and cover its key branches in more detail. Aircraft Checks Starlink Sightings"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-assisted-ufo-sigh-96e9bc-ufo-sighting/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Case Files, covering Sighting Timeline and Witness Interviews.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Case Files","level":3,"text":"All Topics Case Files: All Topics All Topics All Topics Case Files: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Case Files, covering Sighting Timeline and Witness Interviews. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation Explore Topics in Case Files The following pages expand on the main Case Files page and cover its key branches in more detail. Sighting Timeline Witness Interviews"},{"title":"All Topics","url":"/ai-assisted-ufo-sigh-96e9bc-unexplained/","description":"Guide to sub-topic pages of Unresolved Cases, covering GEIPAN Cases and Conflicting Evidence.","breadcrumb":"Can AI Help Explain a Mysterious UFO Sighting? / Unresolved Cases","level":3,"text":"All Topics Unresolved Cases: All Topics All Topics All Topics Unresolved Cases: All Topics Guide to sub topic pages of Unresolved Cases, covering GEIPAN Cases and Conflicting Evidence. AI assisted UFO sighting investigation Explore Topics in Unresolved Cases The following pages expand on the main Unresolved Cases page and cover its key branches in more detail. GEIPAN Cases Conflicting Evidence"}]};
